Subject: [PATCH] build: take the upstream kernel from a declared pekit input
Origin: other, Peios catalogue qualification review
Upstream-Status: Pending [pekit input support, PEI-1137]

build:upstream imported the pristine Linux tree from org.kernel.linux-source, a
package whose entire payload was one tarball and whose only consumer was this
target. Because it arrived as a peipkg dependency, the Debian reference rung —
which resolves through apt — could never supply it, and the target failed there
with "Cannot open: /usr/src/dist/linux-7.0.9.tar.xz".

The member recipe now declares the tarball as [input.linux]. Pekit fetches it,
verifies kernel.org's detached signature against Greg Kroah-Hartman's release
key coordinator-side, and pins the bytes in pekit.lock, so the archive stays
independently verifiable and identical in both environments. The build itself
stays offline and needs no PGP implementation in the root — there is no gpg in
the Peios catalogue, so a build-root-side check was never an option natively.

The tree is copied through tar because the two rungs disagree about cp: `cp -a`
asks for the security descriptor and the native coreutils fails with ENOSYS
reading one on a host without KACS, and naming attributes does not help because
the vocabularies differ (GNU has `mode` and no `exec`, the native one has `exec`
and no `mode`, and its plain -R preserves nothing). A tar stream carries modes,
symlinks, hardlinks and mtimes identically on both.

Dropping the archive handling drops xz from this target.

diff --git a/pekit.toml b/pekit.toml
index b0e4d3b..57fad71 100644
--- a/pekit.toml
+++ b/pekit.toml
@@ -19,29 +19,34 @@ KERNEL_VERSION = "v7.0.9"
 [build.upstream]
 command = """
 set -eu
-# Import the independently authenticated, lock-pinned upstream source package.
-# Keeping remote acquisition in its own recipe means this build stays offline
-# and Peipkg can account for the exact upstream input in the dependency graph.
-version=${KERNEL_VERSION#v}
-tar -xJf "/usr/src/dist/linux-$version.tar.xz" \
-	--strip-components=1 --directory "$PEKIT_OUT"
+# The pristine upstream tree, declared as [input.linux] on the member recipe.
+# Pekit fetched it, verified kernel.org's signature coordinator-side and pinned
+# its bytes in pekit.lock before this target ran, so the build stays offline and
+# no build root needs a PGP implementation. It previously arrived as a package
+# whose only payload was the tarball, which the Debian reference rung could not
+# deliver at all.
+#
+# Copied through tar rather than cp, because the two rungs disagree about cp.
+# `cp -a` asks for the whole security descriptor and the native coreutils fails
+# with ENOSYS reading one on a host without KACS; naming attributes instead does
+# not help, because the vocabularies differ — GNU has `mode` and no `exec`, the
+# native one has `exec` and no `mode`, and plain -R preserves nothing there. A
+# tar stream carries modes, symlinks, hardlinks and mtimes identically on both,
+# and carries no security descriptors to fail on.
+tar -cf - -C "$PEKIT_INPUT_LINUX" . | tar -xf - -C "$PEKIT_OUT"
 
 # Source-level prep hook: nothing needed today (patches apply to a pristine
 # tree). Config/toolchain prep belongs to the later build stages, not here.
 """
 
-# Exact by design: Peios' patch series is derived against this Linux release.
 [build.upstream.dependencies.peipkg]
 "org.git.kernel.dash"       = "*"
 "dev.peios.peiosutils"      = "*"
-"org.kernel.linux-source"   = "= 7.0.9-1"
 "org.gnu.tar"               = "*"
-"org.tukaani.xz"            = "*"
 
 [build.upstream.dependencies.apt]
 coreutils = "*"
 tar = "*"
-xz-utils = "*"
 
 [build.source]
 needs = ["upstream"]
